## Why This Is Asked

This question tests open-closed principle discipline and practical extensibility in a security-critical path by requiring a clean interface and a small registry. It demonstrates how to grow capabilities without touching core logic.

## Key Concepts

- Strategy pattern
- Registry (factory/DI) for runtime extensibility
- Dependency management and testing considerations
- Performance and error handling trade-offs

## Code Example

```javascript
interface AuthStrategy {
  authenticate(req: any): boolean
}
class ApiKeyStrategy implements AuthStrategy {
  authenticate(req: any) { /* validate key in header */ return true }
}
class OAuthStrategy implements AuthStrategy {
  authenticate(req: any) { /* validate bearer token */ return true }
}

class AuthRegistry {
  private map = new Map<string, AuthStrategy>()
  register(name: string, s: AuthStrategy) { this.map.set(name, s) }
  get(name: string): AuthStrategy { return this.map.get(name)! }
}

// usage
const registry = new AuthRegistry()
registry.register('apiKey', new ApiKeyStrategy())
registry.register('oauth', new OAuthStrategy())

function handleRequest(req: any) {
  const name = req.authMode // 'apiKey' or 'oauth'
  const strat = registry.get(name)
  if (strat.authenticate(req)) { /* proceed */ } else { /* reject */ }
}
```

